# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_task_create(vx_str name, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_task_fork(vx_str name, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_task_exec(vx_handle scratch, vx_handle bootstrap, uint64_t entry,
                       uint64_t sp);
vx_status vx_task_info(vx_handle task, vx_task_summary *out);
vx_status vx_task_info_of(vx_handle task, uint64_t id, uint32_t flags,
                          vx_task_summary *out);
vx_status vx_task_kill(vx_handle task, vx_str msg);
vx_status vx_task_kill_id(vx_handle task, uint64_t id, vx_str msg);
vx_status vx_task_mem_rw(vx_handle task, vx_mem_op *ops, uint32_t count);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A task is an address space and a table of handles: what Plan 9 calls a process, less its threads, which thread(2) makes and starts.
A task runs while it has a live thread.
It ends when its last thread exits, with the empty exit string, or when it is killed, with the kill's; then its handles are closed, its mappings dropped and its page tables freed.
The task object lives on while any handle to it is open, so its exit string can still be read.
Each task has an id, from 1, never reused, and a parent, the task that made it; when a task is destroyed its children pass to its parent.

## Making a task

`vx_task_create` makes a task with nothing in it: no mappings, no handles and no threads.
Its name is <name>, at most 23 bytes, cut at a NUL and to whole runes.
The caller gets a handle to it with every right, and is its parent.
It is filled from outside, with as_map(2) and handle_dup(2) on its handle, and started with `vx_thread_create` and `vx_thread_start`, as vx-rt's loader does.

`vx_task_fork` is the same call with `VX_TASK_FORK`: the new task gets a copy of the caller's memory and of its handle table, made now and charged in full, as POSIX's `fork` wants (see the kernel's design, 01 §9).

- Each mapping is copied to the same address with the same permissions.
  A ring's memory and device memory are not copied: the child finds those addresses unmapped.
  A VMO a pager supplies is shared, not copied, as a file mapped `MAP_SHARED` is.
- Each handle keeps its value and its rights, so what the parent's memory says about its handles holds in the child.
  A handle to the parent itself becomes one to the child.
- The in-task exception handler is the parent's (exception(2)); exception ports, a debugger's binding, watchpoints and I/O ports are not copied.

The copy has no threads: the caller starts one.

## Exec

`vx_task_exec` replaces the calling task's program, keeping the task (ADR-0012).
<scratch> is a task the caller made and filled with the new image and its stack, and never started.
The calling task takes <scratch>'s address space and its name; its old address space goes to <scratch>, which ends with the empty exit string.
Every handle the caller holds is closed except <bootstrap>, which moves to the new program and is given to a new thread as its first argument, at <entry> with stack <sp>.
The calling thread ends.
The in-task exception handler is cleared; exception ports and a debugger's binding stay.
The task keeps its id, its parent and its `EXIT` bindings, which fire only when it really ends.

<bootstrap> is meant to be a channel end holding the spawn message, but any handle with `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` will do; it may not be the caller's own task or <scratch>.
Only a task with one live thread may exec.
On success the call does not return.

## Reading a task

`vx_task_info` fills a `vx_task_summary` for <task>:

: `id`, `name`
  The task's id and name, the name padded with NULs.
: `state`
  `VX_TASK_NEW` before any thread has started, `VX_TASK_RUNNING`, or `VX_TASK_EXITED` once it has been torn down.
: `threads`, `blocked`
  How many threads are live, and how many of those are waiting in a call.
: `mapped`
  The bytes mapped into its address space.
: `exit_len`, `exit`
  Its exit string, once it has ended: `exit_len` bytes, none for success.

`vx_task_info_of` reads instead the task <id> in <task>'s tree: <task> itself or one of its descendants.
With `VX_TASK_NEXT` in <flags>, it reads the task in the tree with the next id after <id>, so a holder of a task handle lists its tree by starting from 0 and asking again from each id, as procfs(4) does.
There is no other way to reach a task: no global lookup.

## Ending a task

`vx_task_kill` ends <task> with <msg> as its exit string (ADR-0010): empty for success, else the reason, in at most `VX_ERRMAX` (128) bytes, cut to whole runes.
Each of its threads exits the next time it heads back to user mode, so a thread blocked in a call is woken with `VX_ERR_KILLED` first.
A task with no live thread, one never started among them, is torn down at once.
Killing a task that is already ending changes nothing and succeeds: the first exit string stands.
A task that kills itself does not return from the call.
`vx_task_kill_id` ends the task <id> in <task>'s tree, as `vx_task_info_of` finds it.

A task's end is seen through a binding of the trigger `VX_TRIGGER_EXIT` on it (port_bind(2)), whose packet's value is the exit string's length: 0 for success.

The kernel writes the exit string itself when a fault no one handles kills a task, in Plan 9's words: `sys: trap: fault read addr=0x0 pc=0x401000`.
A note sent to a task with no handler (thread(2)) ends it with the note.

## Another task's memory

`vx_task_mem_rw` copies between <task>'s memory and the caller's, a debugger's access (dbg(1)).
It does <count> operations, at most 16, each a `vx_mem_op`: <size> bytes, at most 1 MiB, between <address> in the task and <buffer> in the caller, from the caller when <write> is 1, to it when 0.
Each operation's result goes in its own <status>, and the call returns `VX_OK` once it has written them back.

A write to a mapping that is not writable, such as code for a breakpoint, first gives that mapping a private copy of its pages, as `ptrace` does, and the write goes there: the task never gets a writable mapping of its code, and nobody else sharing the VMO sees the write.
A write to a writable mapping goes to the VMO itself.

## Rights

`vx_task_create` needs no handle.
`vx_task_exec` needs `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` on <scratch> and `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` on <bootstrap>; `vx_task_info` needs `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`, `vx_task_kill` `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`, and `vx_task_mem_rw` `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`.
A task's maker holds every right to it, so it may debug what it starts.

# EXAMPLES

Kill every task named `drv-virtio-blk` in a tree, from `tests/user/blktest.c`:

```c
vx_task_summary info = {};
uint64_t id = 0;
int killed = 0;
while (vx_task_info_of(tasks, id, VX_TASK_NEXT, &info) == VX_OK) {
  id = info.id;
  if (memcmp(info.name, "drv-virtio-blk", 15) == 0 &&
      vx_task_kill_id(tasks, id, VX_STR("killed")) == VX_OK)
    killed++;
}
```

